709 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1072 0 R/E(Make a list of all the possible people that could come to support and help you get some rest after the birth.)/Pg 42 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

710 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1073 0 R/E(Explain ahead of time what you will be trying to achieve and ask for specific help from people. People lo to help; they just don't know how to do it most times.)/Pg 42 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

711 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1074 0 R/E(Keep early afternoons free for napping, which is when most of us are at our sleepiest.)/Pg 42 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

712 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1075 0 R/E(Make a note for the door to avoid being woken up during your nap or by unwanted visitors. Something like "New mother and baby sleeping, please don't knock or ring the doorbell, leave deliveries by the door/in this safe place/with neighbours/come back later".)/Pg 42 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

713 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1076 0 R/E(Try to rest. You may have heard the expression sleep when the baby sleeps there is some wisdom in it. If you cannot sleep, you could simply lie down and relax and/or listen to a relaxation track.)/Pg 42 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

714 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1077 0 R/E(Choose your supporters wisely, in that ideally, they should be both helpful and make you feel good. Having a bossy mother in law that irritates you isn't going to make for restful time. Nor is having a relative who expects to be waited upon. If you can afford it, consider hiring a postnatal doula. Or get family and friends to buy you doula vouchers. There are so many reasons for why this can be a game changer.)/Pg 42 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

715 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1078 0 R/E(Get your partner on board with your postnatal need for rest \(if you have a partner\). The first cause of conflict in couples after the birth of a baby is "who does what". Sit down with your partner, and separately write a list of all the chores and who does them. Then sit down and compare notes. Work together through what tasks can be dumped, outsourced, and taken on by the partner. The book "Baby's here! who does what?" by Duncan Fisher is a useful and easy to read resource.)/Pg 42 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

716 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1079 0 R/E(Plan for "one week in bed, one week around the bed/on the sofa".)/Pg 42 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

717 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1080 0 R/E(Batch cook and freeze food, ask for visitors to bring you homemade dishes, get some food delivered, stock up on nutritious snacks.)/Pg 42 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

718 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1081 0 R/E(Ask for gifts for yourself rather than the baby. This can include food deliveries, vouchers for a postnatal doula or a postnatal massage or bodywork, offers to come and look after your baby so you can sleep etc \(check my post on Instagram/Facebook!\))/Pg 42 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

719 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1082 0 R/E(Think about who can hold/feed baby. What would you like?)/Pg 42 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

720 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1083 0 R/E(Plan for the unplanned. You might want to make sure that your plan includes possible curveballs, such as having a long induction or a caesarean, or if you and/or your baby need to stay in hospital for a while.)/Pg 42 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

